    "category": "Living",
    "title": "The Skillet Dinner That Saves a Tuesday",
    "subtitle": "Crisp-edged beans, sweet tomatoes and cool yogurt turn a short ingredient list into a proper meal. Bread is mandatory in spirit.",
    "date": "2026-10-15",
    "read": 6,
    "author": "WILDWIRE Kitchen",
    "image": "images/tomato-bean-skillet.webp",
    "imageAlt": "Blistered tomatoes and white beans in a dark skillet with yogurt, herbs and toasted bread",
    "imageCredit": "AI-generated editorial illustration of the recipe; the dish is a WILDWIRE kitchen test.",
    "body": [
      [
        "A real dinner, even when the day ran long",
        "Some nights ask for a recipe with three pans, twelve specialist ingredients and the emotional bandwidth of a cooking show. This is not that night. This is a skillet, a tin of beans and enough heat to turn tomatoes sweet at the edges.\n\nThe result is generous rather than precious: creamy beans, bright tomato, sharp lemon, cool yogurt and bread that catches the oil. Put it in the middle of the table. Let people tear their own pieces. The meal feels considered without demanding that Tuesday become an occasion."
      ],
      [
        "What you need",
        "This serves two hungry people or three with a side salad.\n\n- 2 tablespoons olive oil, plus a little to finish\n- 1 small yellow onion, thinly sliced\n- 2 garlic cloves, thinly sliced\n- 1 pint cherry tomatoes\n- 2 cans white beans, drained and rinsed\n- 1/2 teaspoon smoked paprika, black pepper and salt\n- 1/3 cup water or stock\n- 1 lemon, zest and juice kept separate\n- 1/2 cup thick plain yogurt\n- A handful of parsley, dill or whatever soft herbs are alive in your fridge\n- Good bread, toasted"
      ],
      [
        "Give the pan ten honest minutes",
        "Warm the oil in a wide skillet over medium-high heat. Add the onion with a pinch of salt and cook until soft and beginning to colour, about five minutes. Add the garlic and paprika; stir for thirty seconds so the kitchen smells like dinner is actually happening.\n\nTip in the tomatoes. Leave them alone for two minutes before turning them. Let a few blister and split; the browned spots are the point. Add the beans, water or stock, pepper and another pinch of salt. Fold gently so the beans stay mostly whole.\n\nSimmer for six to eight minutes, until the liquid turns glossy and the tomatoes slump into it. Press a few tomatoes against the side of the pan to thicken the sauce. Turn off the heat. Add lemon juice and taste. If it feels flat, it probably needs salt or acid; add a little, taste again, and stop when the flavours wake up."
      ],
      [
        "Finish like you meant to make it",
        "Spoon yogurt onto plates or drag it in a wide stripe across the skillet. Add lemon zest, herbs and a thread of olive oil. Toast the bread hard enough to scoop, not so hard that it becomes a weapon.\n\nFor more bite, add chilli flakes with the garlic. For a deeper meal, fold in a handful of spinach during the last minute or serve with a bitter green salad. No fresh herbs? A little chopped scallion works. No tomatoes worth buying? Use a can of chopped tomatoes and simmer a few minutes longer; the texture will be softer, the dinner still good."
      ],
      [
        "The useful part is the repeat",
        "This is a flexible template, not a kitchen exam. Beans bring substance; tomatoes bring sweetness and acidity; yogurt cools the pan; bread makes sure nothing good is left behind. Keep those roles in mind and the exact vegetables can change with the week.\n\nDouble the skillet if you want lunch tomorrow. Let leftovers cool, refrigerate them in a covered container, and reheat until steaming hot; add a splash of water if the sauce tightens. Keep the yogurt and herbs separate until serving so they stay fresh.\n\nThat is a good weeknight bargain: one pan, ingredients you can actually find, and a dinner worth sitting down for. Put the phone away for the first few bites if you can. Not as a rule. Just because the crisp bread, warm beans and cold yogurt deserve your attention."
      ]
    ],
