    "title": "Wear It Like You Mean It",
    "subtitle": "A shared point of view starts with clothes that fit your real days—not a matching outfit or a shopping spree.",
    "date": "2026-10-16",
    "read": 5,
    "author": "WILDWIRE Editorial",
    "image": "images/edition-20261016-style-fixed.webp",
    "imageAlt": "A Black woman and East Asian man walk with quiet confidence in thoughtful city clothes after rain.",
    "imageCredit": "AI-generated editorial image. The people and street scene are illustrative, not documentary.",
    "body": [
      [
        "Style is the signal you choose",
        "They meet outside a small restaurant after work. One has a long charcoal coat, the other a dark knit under a clean jacket. Nothing matches. Nothing is trying too hard. The connection is in the level of care: shoes looked after, proportions considered, clothes chosen for the lives they actually lead.\n\nThat is a better starting point for dressing with a partner than matching colors or building a joint uniform. A shared style can mean you both value ease, good fit, texture, repair or a little drama. The expression should still belong to each person. You are not dressing as a couple; you are two people arriving in the same frame with your own point of view."
      ],
      [
        "Start with the week, not the fantasy",
        "Lay out what a normal week asks of you: work, training, caregiving, evenings out, weather, transit and time on your feet. The wardrobe that works is shaped by those hours. A beautiful jacket that stays on a hanger because it is impractical is a poor investment, however good it looked online.\n\nA 2026 study in Psychology & Marketing tested “cost per wear” as a way to compare clothing options. Across six preregistered experiments, communicating the expected cost per wear shifted preferences toward some higher-quality garments, especially when items could be compared. The authors also caution that the effect depends on context and options; a number cannot tell you everything about labor, materials, repairability or whether the piece feels like you [1]."
      ],
      [
        "Build a two-person point of view",
        "Try this in twenty minutes, using what you already own.\n\n1. **Choose three shared words.** Each person picks words independently—perhaps “grounded,” “sharp” and “useful.” Compare lists and keep the overlap. These words describe the atmosphere, not a dress code.\n2. **Name each person’s non-negotiables.** One might need shoes for a long commute; the other may want room to move. Keep those needs intact.\n3. **Find one repeated detail.** It could be a dark neutral, a strong belt, a crisp collar, a worn leather texture or a clean silhouette. It is a thread, not a uniform.\n4. **Build one complete outfit each.** Start with a piece already worn often. Add layers for the actual weather and the actual plan. Take a quick photo so the combination is easy to repeat.\n5. **Use the chair test.** Sit, walk, reach, climb stairs. If the outfit only works while standing still, edit it.\n6. **Write down the gap.** If something is missing, name the exact job it needs to do before shopping. “A rain-ready shoe for the walk to work” is a useful brief. “A whole new look” is not."
      ],
      [
        "Let the numbers ask a better question",
        "Cost per wear is easy to calculate: divide the purchase price by a realistic estimate of wears. A $120 overshirt worn 100 times costs $1.20 per wear before care and repair. That does not make it automatically better than a cheaper piece; the estimate is only useful if the garment survives and you enjoy wearing it.\n\nResearch into clothing use also reminds us that lifespan is not just a materials question. A 2020 international wardrobe survey examined wearing and washing practices across five countries and found that how garments are used and cared for affects their active life [2]. Treat that as a reason to learn the care label, air garments between wears when appropriate, mend a loose button early and buy for fit—not a promise that one fabric or price point is always sustainable."
      ],
      [
        "Keep the individual in the picture",
        "If one person loves color and the other lives in black, the shared point of view can be restraint in fit, not identical palettes. If one wants a bold look for a night out, the other can dress in a way that feels equally considered without becoming a prop. Compliments should sound like recognition, not permission: “That looks like you” carries more respect than “Now we match.”\n\nStart with an outfit you already trust. Ask what makes it work, then give the other person the same space to answer. The aim is not to become a styled pair for strangers. It is to leave the house feeling more like yourselves—and a little more deliberate about the impression you leave."
      ]
    ],
